DEF3="Mutualism: both organisms benefit::mutualism,both benefit,both|Parasitism: the parasite benefits and the host is harmed::parasitism,parasite,host,harmed|Commensalism: one benefits and the other is neither helped nor harmed::commensalism,unaffected,neither"
ADAPT="Adaptation is a feature of body or behaviour that helps an organism survive and reproduce in its habitat::feature,body,behaviour,survive,habitat|Structural: a physical body feature such as thick bark, deep roots or waterproof skin::structural,physical,bark,roots,skin|Behavioural: an action such as migrating, hibernating or hunting at night::behavioural,action,migrating,hibernating,night"
QA=[
S("Define a landform and name the four main types",5,"A landform is a natural physical feature of the Earth's surface. The four types are the plateau, plain, mountain and hills, and valley.",
  "A landform is a natural physical feature of the Earth's surface::natural,physical,feature,surface|Plateau::plateau|Plain::plain|Mountain and hills::mountain,hills,hill|Valley::valley"),
S("Describe the Kwahu Plateau",3,"The Kwahu Plateau is in the Eastern Region and rises to about 760 metres above sea level. It is cooler than the lowlands, often has more rainfall than the plains, and is windy.",
  "It is in the Eastern Region::eastern|It rises to about 760 metres::760,metres|It is cooler and receives more rainfall than the plains, with strong wind::cooler,cool,rainfall,wind,windy"),
S("Name three plants found on Ghana's savanna plains",3,"Baobab (enormous water-storing trunk), acacia (flat umbrella-shaped canopy), shea tree (produces shea nuts), and tall grasses that turn golden-brown in the dry season.",
  "Baobab with an enormous trunk that stores water::baobab,trunk|Acacia with a flat umbrella-shaped canopy::acacia,umbrella,flat|Shea tree or grasses::shea,grass,grasses"),
S("Explain what makes the Atewa Range special for biodiversity",2,"It is one of Ghana's most biodiverse forest reserves, home to species found nowhere else on Earth.","It is one of Ghana's most biodiverse forest reserves::biodiverse,biodiversity,forest reserve|It has species found nowhere else on Earth::nowhere else,endemic,only"),
S("Describe the typical animals found in a river valley",4,"Hippopotamuses, Nile crocodiles, herons, kingfishers, fish eagles, fish (tilapia, catfish, Nile perch), frogs, monitor lizards, freshwater turtles, monkeys and bushbuck.",
  "Hippopotamus::hippo,hippopotamus|Nile crocodile::crocodile|Birds such as herons, kingfishers or fish eagles::heron,herons,kingfisher,kingfishers,fish eagle|Fish such as tilapia, catfish or Nile perch, or frogs, turtles::tilapia,catfish,perch,frog,frogs,turtle,turtles,monitor"),
S("Explain what an epiphyte is",2,"An epiphyte is a plant that grows on other plants without harming them, for example orchids and mosses on branches in the hill forests.","A plant that grows on another plant without harming it::grows on,other plants,without harming,not harm|An example such as an orchid or moss::orchid,orchids,moss,mosses,fern"),
S("Describe how the climate of Ghana's northern plains differs",2,"The northern plains have a long dry season and a shorter rainy season, with high temperatures and lower rainfall, while the hill forests receive high rainfall and are significantly cooler.",
  "Plains: long dry season, high temperatures and low rainfall::dry,hot,high temperature,low rainfall,lower rainfall|Hills: high rainfall and cooler::high rainfall,cooler,cool,humid"),
S("What is gallery forest and why is it important",2,"Gallery forest is dense tree growth along river banks. It provides shade and stabilises the banks, preventing erosion.","Dense tree growth along river banks::dense,trees,river banks,banks|It provides shade and stabilises the banks::shade,stabilises,stabilizes,erosion"),
S("Explain why the soil in river valleys is typically more fertile",2,"Floods deposit soil over centuries, so valley soil is deep, fertile and moist, while plateau soils are thinner and more exposed.","Valley soil is deposited by floods over centuries, so it is deep and moist::deposited,floods,deep,moist|Plateau soils are thinner and more exposed::thin,thinner,exposed"),
S("Name two animals found on Ghana's savanna plains",4,"Cattle, which walk long distances for pasture and water; grasshoppers, whose camouflage matches the dry grass; snakes, which are heat-resistant; kestrels, which hunt rodents in open grassland.",
  "An animal found on the savanna::cattle,goat,goats,sheep,antelope,fox,guinea fowl,snake,snakes,grasshopper,insects,kestrel,termite|A feature that helps it survive (e.g. walks long distances, camouflage, heat resistance)::distance,water,camouflage,colour,heat,resistant,hunt|A second animal::cattle,goat,goats,sheep,antelope,fox,guinea fowl,snake,snakes,grasshopper,insects,kestrel,termite|Its feature::distance,water,camouflage,colour,heat,resistant,hunt"),
S("Explain why different landforms in Ghana support different types of plants and animals",3,"Each landform has a different climate, soil and water supply (cooler and windy plateau, hot and dry plain, humid hills, wet fertile valley), and each organism is adapted to its own habitat, so only suited plants and animals live there.",
  "Landforms differ in climate, soil and water supply::climate,soil,water,rainfall,temperature|Organisms are adapted to their particular habitat::adapted,adaptation,habitat,suited|So only organisms suited to that landform can survive there::survive,suited,only"),
S("A wildlife photographer visits Ghana",3,"The valley (hippopotamus, crocodiles, fish eagles, kingfishers) and the hill forests (monkeys, chameleons, hornbills, butterflies) because they are the richest in life; any two with specific organisms is acceptable.",
  "Names two landforms::valley,hills,hill,forest,plain,plateau,mountain|A reason such as rich in life or biodiversity::rich,biodiverse,variety,diverse|Specific organisms found there::hippo,crocodile,monkey,chameleon,hornbill,eagle,antelope,baobab"),
S("A farmer on the northern plains wants to plant trees",4,"Baobab, because its trunk stores water and its thick bark resists fire; acacia, because its small leaflets reduce water loss and thorns protect it; shea, because it tolerates the plain and gives shea nuts for oil and butter.",
  "A suitable plain tree such as baobab, acacia or shea::baobab,acacia,shea|A reason such as it stores water, reduces water loss, resists fire or is useful::stores water,water loss,fire,drought,shea nuts|A second suitable tree::baobab,acacia,shea|A reason for the second::stores water,water loss,fire,drought,shea nuts"),
S("Define adaptation and explain the difference between a structural adaptation",4,"An adaptation is a feature of an organism's body or behaviour that helps it survive and reproduce in its habitat. Structural: a physical feature, e.g. the thick bark of the baobab. Behavioural: an action, e.g. travelling long distances for water in the dry season.",ADAPT),
S("Describe three structural adaptations of the baobab tree",3,"It has an enormous trunk that stores water for the dry season, thick fire-resistant bark that protects it from bush fires, and its roots help it reach water.",
  "Enormous trunk that stores water::trunk,stores,water storage|Thick fire-resistant bark::bark,fire|A third feature such as roots or storing thousands of litres::roots,litres,deep"),
S("Explain how the roots of riverside trees in valleys are adapted differently",2,"Riverside tree roots reach deep into the bank to reach water and anchor against erosion, or prop roots spread into the mud for stability and oxygen, while plateau trees use deep tap roots to reach groundwater in rocky soil.",
  "Valley roots anchor in the bank or mud and get oxygen (prop roots)::anchor,prop,mud,bank,oxygen|Plateau trees have deep tap roots to reach groundwater::tap root,tap roots,deep,groundwater"),
S("Describe three features of the hippopotamus",3,"Its eyes, ears and nostrils are on top of its head so it can sense the world while submerged; its thick, nearly hairless skin secretes a red-tinted fluid that acts as sunscreen and antibiotic; it spends days in the water and grazes at night.",
  "Eyes, ears and nostrils on top of the head::eyes,ears,nostrils,top|Thick skin secreting a fluid that is sunscreen and antibiotic::skin,fluid,sunscreen,antibiotic|Lives in the water by day::water,submerged,aquatic"),
S("Explain how epiphytes survive without roots in soil",2,"Epiphytes such as orchids and bromeliads have specialised roots that absorb moisture from the humid air, and they grow on tree branches where they get light.","Specialised roots absorb moisture from the humid air::absorb,moisture,humid,air|They grow on tree branches to get light::branches,light,trees"),
S("Describe the key adaptations of the crocodile",3,"Its streamlined body and powerful tail make it a fast swimmer; it can hold its breath and lie submerged and motionless; its eyes and nostrils are raised on its head so it can watch while almost invisible.",
  "Streamlined body and powerful tail for fast swimming::streamlined,tail,swim,swimmer|Holds its breath and lies submerged and motionless::breath,submerged,motionless|Eyes and nostrils raised on the head::eyes,nostrils,elevated,raised,top"),
S("Explain how savanna grasses survive the long dry season",2,"They die back above the ground, but their root systems survive underground and burst into growth when the first rains arrive.","They die back above ground in the dry season::die back,above,ground|The roots survive and grow again when rain returns::root,roots,rains,regrow,survive"),
S("Describe two adaptations of chameleons",2,"Laterally compressed body and tong-like feet to grip thin branches; independently moving eyes for all-round vision; colour-changing skin for camouflage.",
  "Compressed body and gripping feet for thin branches::compressed,grip,feet,branches|Independently moving eyes or colour-changing skin::eyes,two directions,colour,camouflage"),
S("Explain why tall forest trees in Ghana's hill ranges have buttress roots",2,"Buttress roots are large flange-like extensions that spread widely to anchor the tall tree in the shallow forest soil and to absorb nutrients from the thin leaf litter.","They anchor the tall tree in shallow soil::anchor,shallow,support|They absorb nutrients from the leaf litter::absorb,nutrients,leaf litter"),
S("Compare the wing shape of a savanna bird",2,"Plains birds such as kestrels have long pointed wings for speed in open air, while forest birds have rounded wings for manoeuvring through dense vegetation.","Savanna birds have long pointed wings for speed in open air::long,pointed,speed,open|Forest birds have rounded wings for manoeuvring through dense vegetation::rounded,manoeuvr,maneuver,dense"),
S("Describe two ways in which the natural skin of the hippopotamus protects it",2,"It secretes a red-tinted fluid that acts as a sunscreen and protects against sunburn, and as an antibiotic that protects against infection.","Protects against sunburn (sunscreen)::sunscreen,sunburn|Protects against infection (antibiotic)::antibiotic,infection"),
S("A scientist discovers a new animal on the Kwahu Plateau",3,"It is probably a bird of prey like an eagle: it eats small rodents, hunts by soaring on rising warm air and spotting prey from far away, camouflaged by its brown-and-gold feathers; it would have sharp curved talons and a hooked beak.",
  "It eats small rodents (it is a bird of prey)::rodent,rodents,prey,eagle|It soars on rising warm air and spots prey with sharp eyesight::soar,soars,thermals,eyesight,warm air|Sharp curved talons and a hooked beak::talons,claws,beak"),
S("Climate change is causing Ghana's northern plains to become hotter and drier",3,"Water and pasture would be scarcer, so animals would need to travel further or may die; heat could affect animals that cannot tolerate it. Survival adaptations include heat resistance, going longer without water or being active only in the cooler morning and evening.",
  "Water and pasture become scarcer::water,pasture,scarce,less|Animals may travel further, die or leave::travel,die,leave,migrate,decline|An adaptation such as heat resistance or long periods without water::heat,resistance,without water,cooler,morning,evening"),
S("Define mutualism, parasitism and commensalism",6,"Mutualism: both organisms benefit (bees and flowers). Parasitism: the parasite benefits and the host is harmed (ticks on cattle). Commensalism: one benefits and the other is unaffected (cattle egrets and cattle).",DEF3+"|Examples such as bees and flowers, ticks and cattle, cattle egrets::bees,ticks,egret,egrets,cattle,striga,orchid"),
S("Explain the mutualistic relationship between leguminous plants and Rhizobium",3,"Rhizobium bacteria live in nodules on the roots of legumes and change nitrogen gas from the air into a form the plant can use; the plant gives the bacteria carbohydrates. Farmers benefit because legumes improve the soil for later crops.",
  "Rhizobium bacteria live in nodules on legume roots::nodules,roots,rhizobium|They convert nitrogen from the air into a form the plant can use::nitrogen,convert,air|The plant gives them carbohydrates::carbohydrates|Legumes improve the soil for later crops::soil,improve,subsequent,crops"),
S("Describe the relationship between bees and flowering plants",3,"It is mutualism. Bees collect nectar for food and carry pollen from flower to flower, enabling fertilisation, so both benefit.","Mutualism::mutualism|The bee gets nectar (food)::nectar,food|The plant gets its pollen transferred, so it can reproduce::pollen,pollinat,fertilis,reproduce"),
S("Explain how the Striga plant is a parasite of cereal crops",3,"Striga (witchweed) attaches its roots to the roots of maize, sorghum and millet and withdraws water, nutrients and carbohydrates, reducing maize yields by 40 to 80 percent in heavily infested fields.",
  "It attaches its roots to the roots of cereal crops::attaches,roots,cereal,maize,sorghum,millet|It withdraws water, nutrients and carbohydrates::water,nutrients,carbohydrates|It reduces yields by 40 to 80 percent::yield,yields,40,80"),
S("Describe the cattle egret-cattle relationship",3,"Cattle egrets follow cattle and catch grasshoppers and frogs disturbed by the cattle. The egret benefits but the cattle are neither helped nor harmed, so it is commensalism, not mutualism.",
  "Egrets follow cattle and catch insects and frogs they disturb::follow,catch,grasshoppers,frogs,insects|The cattle are unaffected::unaffected,neither,not helped,not harmed|So it is commensalism because only one benefits::commensalism,one benefits"),
S("What is Plasmodium and how does it harm its human host",3,"Plasmodium is a parasitic organism that causes malaria. It lives and reproduces in the human body and causes fever and organ damage, and can kill. It is carried by the female Anopheles mosquito.",
  "A parasitic organism that causes malaria::parasite,parasitic,malaria|It causes fever and organ damage and can kill::fever,organ,die,death|Carried by the female Anopheles mosquito::anopheles,mosquito,female"),
S("Explain why mutualistic associations are critical to the functioning of ecosystems",3,"Mutualism creates networks of interdependence. If the pollinators such as bees are removed, flowering plants cannot reproduce and the whole food web above them can collapse.",
  "Mutualism creates networks of interdependence::interdependence,depend,dependent|If bees are removed, flowering plants cannot reproduce::bees,pollinators,reproduce,pollination|The whole food web can collapse::collapse,food web"),
S("Explain why parasites do not usually kill their hosts immediately",2,"A living host keeps providing food for the parasite, but a dead host provides no further benefit, so the parasite would also lose its food and home.","A living host keeps providing benefit::living,alive,continue,ongoing|A dead host is useless to the parasite::dead,useless,no further benefit,no benefit"),
S("Describe the relationship between epiphytic orchids and forest trees",3,"It is commensalism. The orchid uses the tree's height and structure to reach light but takes no nutrients from the tree, so the orchid benefits and the tree is unaffected.",
  "Commensalism::commensalism|The orchid benefits by using the tree's height to get light::benefits,light,height,structure|The tree is unaffected because nothing is taken from it::unaffected,not harmed,no nutrients,does not take"),
S("Explain how parasitism can sometimes have an indirect positive effect on a population",2,"Parasites often affect sick or weak individuals most, which indirectly improves the overall health of the host population and keeps populations in check.","Parasites preferentially affect sick or weak individuals::sick,weak,individuals|So the overall health of the host population improves::health,healthier,improves,populations in check"),
S("Describe how the introduction of an invasive parasite",3,"Organisms with no evolved resistance suffer catastrophic damage. Striga weed in northern Ghana is devastating because local maize varieties are not evolved to resist it, and its spread has increased with soil degradation.",
  "Organisms have no evolved resistance, so the damage is catastrophic::resistance,catastrophic,devastating|Example: the Striga weed in northern Ghana::striga|Local maize varieties are not resistant::maize,varieties,not evolved"),
S("A farmer in the Upper East Region notices that her cowpeas",3,"Cowpeas are legumes with Rhizobium bacteria in their root nodules, which change nitrogen from the air into a form plants can use, enriching the soil. She should grow legumes in rotation with maize and other crops to improve the soil.",
  "Rhizobium bacteria in cowpea root nodules::rhizobium,bacteria,nodules|They convert nitrogen from the air into a usable form, improving the soil::nitrogen,converted,improve,soil|Recommend planting legumes before or alongside other crops (rotation)::rotation,legumes,before,plant,cowpeas"),
S("A school science club is studying a forest near their community",3,"(a) Moss on tree bark is commensalism: the moss benefits, the tree is unaffected. (b) Ticks on a deer are parasitism: the tick feeds on blood and the deer is harmed. (c) Bees visiting wildflowers are mutualism: the bees get nectar and the flowers are pollinated.",
  "(a) Moss on bark: commensalism (the tree is unaffected)::commensalism,unaffected|(b) Ticks on a deer: parasitism (tick feeds, deer harmed)::parasitism,blood,harmed|(c) Bees and wildflowers: mutualism (nectar and pollination)::mutualism,nectar,pollination,pollinat"),
]
